## The log format — confirmed and locked

```
TIMESTAMP | actor -> process | path | change
```

Every field, every case:

```
# Known actor, any change except ownership
2026-03-13 16:05:22 | vamsiva -> nano   | /opt/scripts/backup.sh | new file
2026-03-13 16:05:45 | vamsiva -> chmod  | /opt/scripts/backup.sh | mode: 0644 → 0755
2026-03-13 16:09:03 | vamsiva -> vim    | /opt/scripts/backup.sh | size: 842 → 1031
2026-03-13 16:18:44 | vamsiva -> rm     | /opt/scripts/temp.sh   | deleted

# Ownership change — actor slot shows old_owner -> new_owner
# The change_desc also records it explicitly
2026-03-13 16:07:10 | root -> chown     | /opt/scripts/backup.sh | owner: 1001:1001 → 0:0

# chattr — shows only what changed, not full flag string
2026-03-13 16:12:20 | root -> chattr    | /opt/scripts/backup.sh | chattr: +i
2026-03-13 17:02:11 | unknown           | /opt/secure/critical.bin | chattr: +i ---> -i

# Unknown actor — single field, no arrow
2026-03-13 17:02:11 | unknown           | /opt/secure/critical.bin | chattr: +i ---> -i
```

One thing I want to lock down before writing — the chattr format. Your spec shows two styles:

```
chattr: +i              ← flag was added (simple case)
chattr: +i ---> -i      ← flag was set then unset (poller caught net change)
```

With auditd replacing the poller, we catch chattr in real-time via ioctl. So we'll always know the direction — we won't need the `+i ---> -i` style anymore. Every chattr event will be either `+i` or `-i` individually. Is that acceptable, or do you want to keep the `---> ` style for when both happen close together?
